/* DISCOVERY (why b1 is large and negative): §41 closes with "Not established. Why b1 is large and
   negative on a setpoint-tracked zone ... no derivation here fixes the magnitude." This fixes it,
   and refutes the guess that was standing in for it.

   A proportional tracking loop puts the log on an AFFINE MANIFOLD x = c + m*u with m = -1/Kp. On
   that manifold the four coefficients of the control-affine class dxdt = d + a*x + (b0 + b1*x)*u are
   NOT separately identified -- but b1 alone IS, because it is the only one carrying the u^2 term.
   The identity is b1 = C/m = -Kp*C with C the curvature of the observed response in the action, so
   the sign is decided by the curvature and the MAGNITUDE by the controller: a tighter loop amplifies
   whatever curvature exists. The exact inversion of the usual reading, where a is the trusted pole
   and b1 the fragile interaction. proofs/closed_loop_attribution.v proves the matching identity. */
display2d : false$

/* (1) the manifold. A proportional loop u = Kp*(r - x) + u0 inverts to x = c + m*u exactly. */
loop  : u = Kp*(r - x) + u0$
manif : solve(loop, x)[1]$
print("(1) closed loop x =", rhs(manif))$
print("    residual vs c + m*u with m = -1/Kp, c = r + u0/Kp =",
      ratsimp( rhs(manif) - ((r + u0/Kp) + (-1/Kp)*u) ), "  (=0)")$

/* (2) the fitted class restricted to the manifold, collected in the action. */
fit    : d + a*x + (b0 + b1*x)*u$
onman  : expand( subst(x = c + m*u, fit) )$
c0 : ratsimp( coeff(onman, u, 0) )$
c1 : ratsimp( coeff(onman, u, 1) )$
c2 : ratsimp( coeff(onman, u, 2) )$
print("(2) on the manifold  dxdt = ", c0, " + (", c1, ")*u + (", c2, ")*u^2")$

/* (3) MATCHING against an observed response A + B*u + C*u^2. The u^2 coefficient involves b1 alone,
   so b1 is pinned; the other two equations leave a two-parameter family in (d, a, b0). */
print("(3) b1 from the u^2 match:  b1 =", ratsimp( solve(c2 = C, b1)[1] ))$
print("    residual vs C/m =", ratsimp( rhs(solve(c2 = C, b1)[1]) - C/m ), "  (=0)")$
print("    with m = -1/Kp:  b1 =", ratsimp( subst(m = -1/Kp, C/m) ), "  (= -Kp*C)")$
print("    the other two equations, d + a*c - A =", ratsimp(c0 - A),
      " and  a*m + b0 + b1*c - B =", ratsimp(c1 - B),
      "  (two equations, three unknowns d,a,b0: NOT identified)")$

/* (4) sign and magnitude. Kp > 0, so b1 < 0 exactly when the response is CONVEX in the action, and
   |b1| grows linearly with the loop gain -- a tighter tracker reports a bigger interaction from the
   same physics. That is the magnitude §41 asked for. */
print("(4) d|b1|/dKp at fixed C =", ratsimp( diff(-Kp*C, Kp) ), "  (= -C: linear in the loop gain)")$

/* (5) the numerical anchor. §41 measured b1 = -0.3779 on the hydronic case; Kp = 2.6 with curvature
   C = 0.1454 reproduces it. Both are inputs here, not fitted -- the point is that the identity has a
   solution in a plausible range, and chc.dynamics_id.closed_loop_gain_attribution measures it. */
print("(5) -Kp*C at Kp=2.6, C=0.1454 =", float( subst([Kp = 2.6, C = 0.1454], -Kp*C) ),
      "  (§41 reported -0.3779)")$

/* (6) REFUTATION of the standing guess. plans/24 proposed b1 = -1/Kp from the simultaneity slope.
   That is the slope of the MANIFOLD, m, not the interaction coefficient: b1 = C/m, so -1/Kp would
   require C = 1/Kp^2, a coincidence rather than an identity. The simultaneity slope is a real and
   useful quantity -- it identifies the CONTROLLER -- but it is not b1. */
print("(6) -1/Kp = C/m forces C =", ratsimp( solve(-1/Kp = C/(-1/Kp), C)[1] ),
      "  (a constraint on the plant, not an identity)")$

/* (7) what restores identification: variation OFF the manifold. With x = c + m*u + e the u^2 term is
   no longer the only place b1 can live, the design regains full rank, and a and b0 become
   identified -- with a variance that scales as 1/var(e). Exploration is the budget. */
offman : expand( subst(x = c + m*u + e, fit) )$
print("(7) the e-carrying terms are", ratsimp( offman - subst(e = 0, offman) ),
      "  (= e*(a + b1*u): vanishes with the exploration, and takes a and b1's separation with it)")$
